Historical v1 standalone modifications:
- Combined private implementation into one .c file and added one public .h.
- Removed Zephyr kernel, mutex, device, flash, logging, Kconfig and errno dependencies.
- Added partition-relative synchronous callbacks with normalized error results.
- Added geometry, reserved-ID, size/narrowing, alignment and I/O bounds checks.
- Added explicit little-endian metadata/CRC encoding and small read-only CRC tables.
- Compile-time optional small hashed lookup cache; no heap or full key index.
- Propagate sector-close and early startup read failures; invalidate state on I/O errors.
- Strict full-value/status-based read API; corrected historical-version boundary handling.
- Same-value writes repair a damaged stored CRC rather than skipping that write.
- Harden close-ATE bounds; use unsigned sector-address shifts and sector-relative cleanup.
- Linear backward orphan-data scan in startup; safe O(1) current-sector availability query.
- Explicit destructive format only; no automatic full-format on mount failure.
- All instance operations require application-level serialization.

v2 redesign (2026-09-30):
- Replaced the sector ring/packed streaming GC with two-bank snapshot publication.
- Changed the on-flash format: complemented metadata, an independent record commit
  block, duplicate complemented bank headers, and ID/length-bound payload CRC32.
- Preserve reservations after interrupted programming; reject invalid committed
  metadata rather than falling back to old records or resurrecting deletions.
- Check live-data capacity before erase and verify payload CRC during GC copying.
- Optional temporary GC filter, fixed-geometry bootloader and read-only builds.
- Full/boot/read-only media interoperability and broader unordered NOR fault tests.
- The v1 byte-image compatibility claim no longer applies. Upstream remains a
  logical-value reference and a source of legacy images that v2 must reject.

Tests/upstream contains the two original source files listed above UNMODIFIED.
Its include/ref_shim.h is a TEST-ONLY host adapter for independent execution of
that upstream algorithm; it is not Zephyr and is not a production dependency.
New tests, build scripts, example and the standalone interface are supplied under
Apache-2.0. No Zephyr board-level execution, hardware certification, or universal
power-failure guarantee is represented by the host test results.
